## Rust

### Formats

<!-- Project-specific / Rust-Formats -->

### Names

- Use `snake_case` for functions, variables, and modules; `UpperCamelCase` for types, traits, and enum variants; `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for constants and statics.
- Wrap a primitive in a newtype to name the invariant it carries (`struct OrderId(String)`), instead of passing a bare `String` or `u64` everywhere.
- Name a trait for the capability it grants (`Cache`, `Clock`); never with an `I`- prefix or `Trait` suffix.
- Give every enum variant a name that reads as a state (`Shipped { at: Timestamp }`), not a flag hiding inside a struct.
- Never write a vague name (`data`, `info`, `item`, `temp`) or a bare-verb function with no object (`handle`, `process`); a loop index, closure parameter, or `_` for a discarded value may stay short, nothing else should.
- Never number or version a name instead of replacing it (`user2`, `_old`); never suffix a type with a noise word (`Manager`, `Helper`, `Util`) or name it after a verb (`ProcessOrder`).
<!-- Project-specific / Rust-Names -->

### Packages

- Default every item to private; use `pub(crate)` for cross-module use inside the crate, `pub` only for the crate's real public surface.
- Give each concept its own module under `src/`, re-exported from `lib.rs`; do not mirror folders into a `mod` tree nobody reads.
- Keep a trait beside the module that owns its concept; put a second implementation (a test double, an alternate backend) in its own module.
- Never add a catch-all `utils.rs` or `helpers.rs`; name the module for the concept it implements.
<!-- Project-specific / Rust-Packages -->

### Types

- Model a closed set of states as an `enum`, not a struct with a `status: String` field and scattered booleans.
- Implement `From`/`TryFrom` for a conversion instead of a same-named `to_x`/`from_x` free function per type pair.
<!-- Project-specific / Rust-Types -->

### Functions

- Borrow (`&T`, `&mut T`) by default; clone only where ownership must move, or the type is `Copy` and cheap.
- Keep a function's parameter count small; past three related values, group them into a struct.
